# Install ASH

ASH is a Python command-line tool that you install from its GitHub repository. It is
not published on PyPI. The PyPI project named `automated-security-helper` is not
published by this project, so installing or running ASH by that name from PyPI gets that
project's package, not ASH. Always install from the GitHub URL, as on this page.

Installing ASH gives you the `ash` command. The scanners it runs are separate tools:
after installing, run `ash dependencies install`, described in
[Manage scanner tools](../docs/advanced-usage.md).

## Requirements

- Python 3.11 to 3.14. uv and pipx can fetch a matching Python for you.
- git, which the installer uses to fetch the repository.
- Linux (x86-64 or arm64), macOS, or Windows (x86-64). CI installs ASH with pip and
  pipx on Python 3.11 to 3.14 and with uvx on 3.12, on Ubuntu (x86-64 and arm64),
  macOS on Apple silicon, and Windows. Intel Macs and Windows on arm64 are not tested.

Some scanner tools are not available on every platform: see
[Platform limits](../docs/advanced-usage.md#platform-limits).

Always pass a Python version to uv and pipx, as the commands below do. The v3.7.x
releases accept any Python 3 version from 3.11 up, so on a machine with no suitable
Python, uv picks the newest release, 3.15, and the install fails while building a
dependency that does not support 3.15.

Every install on this page includes the `cdk` extra, which the cdk-nag scanner needs.
Without it, cdk-nag shows as MISSING in a project with CloudFormation templates, and the
scan exits 1. See [Extras](#extras).

## Choose an install method

| Method | Use it when | Status |
|---|---|---|
| [`uv tool install`](#install-with-uv) | you want `ash` on your `PATH` in its own environment | recommended |
| [`uvx`](#run-without-installing-uvx) | you want to run ASH without installing it | supported |
| [`pipx`](#install-with-pipx) | you already use pipx | supported |
| [`pip` in a virtual environment](#install-with-pip) | you manage Python environments yourself | supported |
| [Container image](#container-image) | you want every scanner without installing them | build it yourself |
| [Nix mode](#nix-mode) | you use Nix | supported |
| [Homebrew](#homebrew) | you install your tools with Homebrew, on macOS or Linux | supported |
| pre-commit | you want a scan on every commit | see [Run ASH with pre-commit](pre-commit.md) |
| From a clone | you are changing ASH | see [Contribute to ASH](../contributing/index.md) |

## Install with uv

Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/):

=== "Linux and macOS"

    The installer needs curl:

    <!-- doctest: illustrative reason="downloads and runs the uv installer from the network" -->
    ```bash
    curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
    ```

=== "Windows"

    In PowerShell:

    <!-- doctest: illustrative reason="Windows only; downloads and runs the uv installer from the network" -->
    ```powershell
    powershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"
    ```

Open a new terminal so that uv's directory is on your `PATH`, then install ASH. The
command is the same in a POSIX shell and in PowerShell:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
```bash
uv tool install --python 3.12 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}'
ash --version
```

```text
awslabs/automated-security-helper v{{RELEASE}}
```

The install provides three names for the same program: `ash`, `ashv3`, and
`automated-security-helper`. Releases after 3.8.0 add a fourth,
`agentic-security-harness`. On Windows, Git Bash and
MSYS2 ship an unrelated shell named `ash` that can come first on your `PATH`. Use
`ashv3` there.

To move to another version, run the same command with the new tag and add `--force`.

## Run without installing: uvx

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
```bash
uvx --python 3.12 --from 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}' ash --version
```

uvx builds the environment once and reuses it, so later runs start quickly. Every
command needs the full `uvx --from` specification, so you may want a shell alias:

```bash
alias ash="uvx --python 3.12 --from 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}' ash"
```

## Install with pipx

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
```bash
pipx install --python python3.12 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}'
```

Install pipx itself from your package manager, for example `apt install pipx` on
Ubuntu, or with `uv tool install pipx`. On systems whose Python is marked as
externally managed, such as Ubuntu 24.04, pip refuses to install pipx outside a
virtual environment. pipx needs the named Python on your `PATH`.

ASH installs bandit, checkov, semgrep, cfn-lint, and zizmor with uv. It uses the `uv`
that pipx installs into ASH's environment, so a pipx install needs no separate uv.

## Install with pip

Create a virtual environment outside the projects you will scan, activate it, and
install into it. Use any Python 3.11 to 3.14 in place of 3.12. On systems whose Python
is marked as externally managed, such as Ubuntu 24.04, pip installs only into a
virtual environment.

=== "Linux and macOS"

    <!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
    ```bash
    python3.12 -m venv ~/.venvs/ash
    . ~/.venvs/ash/bin/activate
    pip install 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}'
    ash --version
    ```

=== "Windows"

    In PowerShell:

    <!-- doctest: illustrative reason="Windows only; installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
    ```powershell
    py -3.12 -m venv $HOME\.venvs\ash
    & $HOME\.venvs\ash\Scripts\Activate.ps1
    pip install 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}'
    ash --version
    ```

Activate the environment in each new shell before you run `ash`, or run the
environment's `ash` by its path. Either way, ASH installs bandit, checkov, semgrep,
cfn-lint, and zizmor with the environment's own `uv`.

Keep the environment outside the scanned directory. During a scan ASH does not run
tools that it finds inside the directory it is scanning, so scanners installed into a
virtual environment inside the project show as MISSING. See
[Tools inside the scanned directory](../docs/advanced-usage.md#tools-inside-the-scanned-directory).

## Extras

| Extra | Adds | Needed for |
|---|---|---|
| `cdk` | `aws-cdk-lib`, `cdk-nag`, `constructs` | the cdk-nag scanner, which also needs Node.js |
| `symbols` | tree-sitter grammars for Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, and Java | `symbol` in suppressions; see [Suppress findings](suppress-findings.md) and [Symbol resolution](../reference/symbol-resolution.md) |

To install an extra from git, use the direct-reference form `name[extra] @ URL`, as
the commands on this page do. List several extras with commas, as in
`[cdk,symbols]`.

You can also add the cdk packages later: `ash dependencies install --tool cdk-nag`
installs them into ASH's environment, whichever way you installed ASH. A later
`uv tool upgrade` or `uv tool install --force` rebuilds a uv tool environment without
them, so installing the extra with ASH is simpler.

The `symbols` extra is in `main` and not in releases up to 3.7.x. Its tree-sitter
packages publish no wheels for Linux on arm64 with musl (Alpine), so on that platform
the install needs a C compiler.

## Choose how scanner tools are installed

`ash dependencies install` installs each scanner's tool the way its plugin declares
it: Python tools with `uv tool install`, native tools from pinned, digest-verified
release downloads, and a few through `gem` or the system package manager. Five options
change where tools come from. They are options of the command only; no configuration
file or environment variable sets them, so a repository you scan cannot steer the
installer. Each is listed in the [`ash dependencies install` reference](../reference/cli.md#dependencies-install).

### Providers

`--providers uv,pipx,brew` names the install providers this run may use, in order of
preference. The list is both a permission boundary and an order: only the listed
providers may install anything, and for each tool the first listed provider that can
install it wins. A provider that cannot serve a tool is skipped, with its reason
printed. When none of them can, the tool is refused with every reason, nothing runs for
it, and the run exits 1. ASH never substitutes a provider you did not list.

Without `--providers` every provider is permitted and each tool is installed the way
its plugin declares, which is what the command did before the option existed.

| Provider | What it installs |
|---|---|
| `uv` | Python tools in `uv tool` environments, packages into a Python environment, and `uv venv` virtualenvs |
| `pipx` | Python tools in pipx environments. It cannot install into a named virtualenv, and `pipx install --python` takes an executable, a version, or a path, so a version-range request such as `>=3.10,<3.14` is refused |
| `pip` | Packages into one interpreter, or into the virtualenv named by `--virtualenv-path`; `python -m venv` creates it |
| `release-download` | ASH's pinned release downloads for the tools that have one |
| `nix` | The toolchain the repository flake pins, by realizing the dev shell that `--mode nix` runs. One realization serves every tool in the run, and it takes no interpreter request or exact version pin. The realized tools live in the Nix store and are not placed on `PATH` or in the bin directory, so the post-install verdict can read a nix-only tool as missing; a `--mode nix` scan finds them |
| `apt`, `dnf`, `yum`, `zypper`, `brew`, `npm`, `pnpm`, `conda`, `choco`, `gem` | One generic system-package shape: the install command of that manager for the package that provides the tool |
| `go`, `cargo` | Declared and not implemented; they refuse with "not implemented" |
| `custom` | A plugin's own shell or download command, run as declared |

A package manager installs a tool from the name the install-package facet declares for
it. A tool with no declared name for that manager is refused naming the missing row, and
a package manager installs the version it carries, not the range the plugin declares;
the command's output says so. `apt`, `dnf`, `yum` and `zypper` are declared as root-only,
and ASH does not add `sudo`: run as root, or list another provider.

### System tools

`--inherit-system-tools` is the default, and it is the behavior the command has always
had: a tool already on `PATH` or in a system directory counts as present, and an
identical copy of a pinned tool skips its download.

`--no-inherit-system-tools` ignores those copies. A global `checkov` on `PATH` does not
stop the install, and the result counts only what was installed into a location ASH or
its provider manages: uv's tool listing, the virtualenv you named, ASH's own environment,
and the ASH bin directory. A system copy that was set aside is named in the output. Programs
ASH never installs, such as Node.js for cdk-nag, are still found on `PATH`. The option
affects the install and its report only; a scan finds tools exactly as before.

### Virtualenvs

`--virtualenv-path <dir>` installs Python tools into one virtualenv, through the provider,
instead of into a separate environment per tool.

| Path | Create flag | Result |
|---|---|---|
| none | none | Per-tool environments, as before |
| none | either flag typed | Refused (exit 2): the flag does nothing without a path |
| exists, a virtualenv | `--no-create-virtualenv` (default) | Tools install into it. Each tool's interpreter request is checked against the Python version in its `pyvenv.cfg`, and a tool whose request it fails is refused |
| missing | `--no-create-virtualenv` (default) | Refused (exit 3) |
| missing | `--create-virtualenv` | ASH creates it first, with one interpreter request that satisfies every tool's, then installs into it |
| exists, not a virtualenv | either | Refused (exit 3); ASH never overwrites or deletes it |

Use `--no-create-virtualenv` with a virtualenv your own setup step prepared, and
`--create-virtualenv` to have ASH vend one. Native tools and system packages are not
installed into a virtualenv, so the options do not change them.

The virtualenv must sit outside the directory being scanned, existing or created: the
install commands built around it execute its interpreter (`uv pip install --python
<venv python>`, `<venv python> -m pip`), so a virtualenv the scanned tree supplies is
refused (exit 3), with the same opt-in as a forced binary (`ASH_ALLOW_IN_TREE_TOOLS=1`).
Python-environment installs -- packages ASH's own process imports, such as cdk-nag's CDK
libraries -- stay in the environment running ASH rather than the virtualenv, so what the
install reports installed is what the next scan can import.

### Forcing a binary

`--binary-path scanner=/absolute/path` forces one binary for one scanner and skips that
scanner's install commands. Repeat it for each scanner. The path must be absolute, name a
regular executable file rather than a symlink, and sit outside the directory being
scanned. On Windows, which keeps no execute permission, an executable file is one whose
name ends in `.exe`, `.com`, `.bat` or `.cmd`, the files Windows starts as programs.
ASH reads and hashes the file, records the path and its SHA-256 in its install
records under `~/.ash`, and never runs it. The record is read back: the scanners'
dependency validation and later `ash dependencies install` runs resolve the scanner to
the forced path before any PATH or uv lookup, re-checking the file and its SHA-256 each
time, and a record whose file moved or changed is named and set aside rather than
trusted by its path. `--binary-path scanner=` with an empty path removes the record.

The record answers one question for every scanner, whichever way its dependency check
probes: where the scanner's own binary is. A scanner's other requirements still hold
and are checked first, so the force never turns them off: npm-audit and snyk-code
still refuse to run offline, trivy still needs its offline vulnerability database,
cfn-guard its rules files, and actionlint its configured integrations. Three scanners
run Python libraries rather than one binary and never consult the record: cdk-nag
(aws-cdk-lib and cdk-nag plus Node.js), detect-secrets, and mcp-config -- forcing a
path for them records it but satisfies nothing.

## Choose a version to pin

| Reference | What you get |
|---|---|
| `@v{{RELEASE}}` | That release. The release process creates each version tag once and never moves it. |
| a full commit SHA | That commit. The only reference that cannot change. |
| `@v3` | v3.8.0, forever. The retired floating tag: frozen there, it no longer moves with releases. |
| `@main` | The current development state. |

Release tags are lightweight and unsigned, and releases have no signed artifacts. If
you need a reference that cannot change, pin a commit SHA.

Floating major tags are retired: the `v3` tag used to move to each new 3.x release
and is now frozen at v3.8.0, so a `v3` pin never receives updates again -- pin a
release tag instead. A release is not always made from `main`: v3.7.1 is a backport
made on a separate branch, outside `main`'s history. A 3.x release can also include
breaking changes, so read the release notes before upgrading a `<4` pin.

### Install from main

To use a feature that is on `main` before a release includes it, install from `main`:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network" -->
```bash
uv tool install --python 3.12 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@main'
```

`main` reports the same version number as the latest release until the next release
is made, so `ash --version` does not tell the two apart. Pin a commit SHA instead of
`@main` when you need to know which code you ran.

In [Nix mode](#nix-mode), an install of `main` resolves its flake to the latest
release's tag, because that is the version it reports. Set
`ASH_NIX_FLAKE_REF=github:awslabs/automated-security-helper/main`, or the commit you
pinned, so the scanner toolchain matches the code.

## Container image

No ASH image is published. `ash --mode container` builds an image with every scanner
on your machine and scans inside it; it rebuilds the image on every run unless you
pass `--no-build`. `ash container build` builds one to push to your own registry. See
[Run ASH in a container](run-in-a-container.md).

## Nix mode

`ash --mode nix` runs the scan inside a Nix shell whose scanner tools come from
ASH's `flake.nix`. It needs Nix with flakes enabled, on Linux or macOS (on Windows,
inside WSL2). It does not install ASH itself: the scan inside the shell runs the same
ASH you started, so install ASH with one of the methods above first.

The Nix shell supplies the tools of every built-in scanner except cdk-nag, which runs on
the Python environment ASH is installed in, so install ASH with the `cdk` extra for it.
It does not supply ferret-scan or the Snyk CLI, which two alpha plugins run.

The scan inside the shell gets your environment, so it reads the file `ASH_CONFIG` names
first, as a local scan does, and then the file `--config` names. With no `--config`, the
file `ASH_CONFIG` names is the config too, and with neither, the scan uses the config file
it discovers.
In a scan of one directory, ASH resolves that config on the host before the shell starts.
A file that is missing, cannot be read, does not parse, or is not a valid config stops the
scan with exit code 3 before the previous run's output is cleared, and that includes the
file `ASH_CONFIG` names when `--config` names a valid one.

Nix mode is in the latest release; v3.7.x and older reject `--mode nix`, so install a
newer release:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH from GitHub over the network and scans in a Nix shell" -->
```bash
uv tool install --python 3.12 'automated-security-helper[cdk] @ git+https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git@v{{VERSION}}'
ash scan --mode nix
```

Which flake supplies the shell's tools:

- From a source checkout, ASH uses that checkout's `flake.nix`.
- An installed ASH looks for the flake at the release tag matching the version it
  reports, which releases newer than v3.7.x ship, so a release install needs nothing
  set.
- Set `ASH_NIX_FLAKE_REF` to scan with a different flake: a checkout, written `path:`
  followed by its absolute path, or a branch, tag or commit of this repository or a
  fork. An install of `main` reports the latest release's version number and so
  resolves to that release's flake, not `main`'s; set
  `ASH_NIX_FLAKE_REF=github:awslabs/automated-security-helper/main`, or a matching
  commit, so the scanner toolchain matches the code you run (see
  [Install from main](#install-from-main)).

## Homebrew

The repository is a Homebrew tap: its formula is `Formula/ash.rb`. Add the tap and
install the formula by its full name:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs ASH with Homebrew over the network" -->
```bash
brew tap awslabs/automated-security-helper https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper.git
brew install awslabs/automated-security-helper/ash
ash --version
```

Naming the formula in full installs it from a tap you have not trusted, and Homebrew
trusts that one formula as it installs it. To install it by its short name,
`brew install ash`, first run `brew trust awslabs/automated-security-helper`: Homebrew
refuses to load a formula by its short name from a tap you have not trusted. A Homebrew
release without `brew trust` has no such check.

The formula installs the release named on its `url` line, which is the latest release;
it cannot install `main`. It builds ASH on Homebrew's Python 3.12, with the `cdk` extra,
and installs the dependency versions locked in that release's `uv.lock`, each checked
against the sha256 the lock records. ASH's own build requirements, hatchling and the
packages it needs, are resolved from PyPI by version range and are not checked against
a hash. The formula downloads all of them during the install, so the install needs
network access, and building with `HOMEBREW_FORMULA_BUILD_NETWORK=deny` is not
supported. The formula also installs
Node.js, which cdk-nag and npm-audit run on; a `node` already on your `PATH` comes
first. It does not install the scanners that are separate programs, such as grype,
syft, opengrep, and cfn-nag, so run `ash dependencies install` as with the other
methods.

In container mode, a Homebrew install builds its image from the commit the formula
built. When Homebrew built ASH from a source tarball with no git metadata, it builds the
release tag of ASH's version instead and logs a warning that this is a guess.

CI installs the formula, built from the commit under test, with Homebrew on Ubuntu
(x86-64) and on macOS on Apple silicon, then runs `brew test` and scans with the
installed `ash`. Homebrew is not available on Windows, and Linux on arm64 is not tested.

## Uninstall

Remove ASH with the tool that installed it:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="removes software from the reader's machine" -->
```bash
uv tool uninstall automated-security-helper
pipx uninstall automated-security-helper
brew uninstall ash
```

For a pip install, delete the virtual environment. After `brew uninstall`, remove the tap
with `brew untap awslabs/automated-security-helper`.

The scanner tools and data that ASH installed stay behind. To remove them too:

- `~/.ash/`, which holds the binaries from `ash dependencies install`, their install
  records, and the cfn-guard rules. If you installed with `--bin-path` or
  `ASH_BIN_PATH`, the binaries are in that directory instead, and the cfn-guard rules
  are in `share/cfn-guard-rules` next to it.
- The scanners installed as uv tools. Name the ones `uv tool list` shows, because
  `uv tool uninstall` fails for a name that is not installed:
  `uv tool uninstall bandit checkov semgrep cfn-lint zizmor nbconvert`.
- The cfn-nag gem: `gem uninstall cfn-nag`. The gems it depends on stay installed.
- The scanners' own caches, such as `~/.cache/grype` and `~/.cache/trivy`.
- The `.ash/ash_output/` directory that each scan writes in the project it scanned.
